 ### 13e.2 Single-Confirmation Beta Discipline
 One confirming observation (SMH -4.2pct on 7/13 strike day) is not a stable beta estimate. Any cross-asset sensitivity used in fusion topdown scores must carry a confidence tag (n of observations); n=1 betas get half-weight until a second independent confirmation, and a contradicting observation (7/21) forces an immediate re-estimate, not a rationalization.
+
+### 13f. No-View Odds for every level / range / 'does not close above' call (from #69, 2026-09-19 cycle)
+#69 (SPY 705-775 AND QQQ 640-720 AND SMH 480-605 through 9/30, P=0.60) died in TWO sessions - QQQ closed 723.85 on 8/4. QQQ sat 4.7pct under its bound with ~1.5pct/day realized vol and 42 sessions to run; a driftless random walk gives that ONE leg ~0.1, the three-leg compound less. The call needed a ~+0.5 edge over arithmetic and named no mechanism for it. It also broke this file's own rule (separate assets = separate rows). RULE: before ledgering any level or range call, run `python tools/range_odds.py` (spot, band, sessions, daily vol) and write the no-view number into --sensitivity. Stated P within 0.10 of it = fine. 0.10-0.20 off = name the mechanism. More than 0.20 off = needs a DATED catalyst, else re-price. Compound bands are banned - one asset, one row. Short-fuse level calls are where this works: #77 (SMH <605 for 3 sessions, no-view 0.75 vs stated 0.78) and #78 were modest views and both hit; the <=7-day bucket is the book's best horizon (9/11, Brier 0.15).
+
+### 13g. Lagged pass-through uses the WINDOW AVERAGE, never the spot at logging (from #76)
+#76 called the pump average below $4.00 by 8/25 off 'a >20pct Brent collapse in the first week of August' (P=0.68). Brent's sub-80 print lasted two sessions (8/4-8/5: 79.4); it was 88 by 8/10 and 94 by 8/21; AAA never went below $4.00 and August CPI energy came in +2.1pct m/m, headline +0.4 - the ORIGINAL August-hot thesis (#32, HIT) was right and the self-downgrade was the error. RULES: (1) a pass-through call is priced off the AVERAGE of the input over the lag window (pump prices ~2-3 weeks of crude; CPI energy = the month-average pump price vs the prior month-average), so a 2-session spike or dip moves it far less than it moves the headline; (2) never downgrade a lag-thesis on less than ~1 lag-length of contrary input; (3) when the engine holds two rows that contradict each other (#32 vs #76), that is a hedge, not two insights - say which one it believes and price the other below 0.50.
+
+### 13h. Relative-value calls in war tape: the high-vol leg wins the reversal (from #74)
+#74 (XLE outperforms USO over 5 sessions, P=0.75) lost by six points: USO +10.2pct vs XLE +4.1pct as Brent reversed. USO ran ~3.8pct/day vol vs XLE ~1.25pct. A pair call between a 3-beta and a 1-beta expression of the same underlying is simply a DIRECTION call on the underlying, levered 3:1 - here, a bet that crude keeps falling, i.e. leg-extrapolation (doctrine 16.15). The refining-margin mechanism was real but second-order. RULE: for any RV row, compute the vol ratio; if it exceeds ~2, restate the row as the direction call it really is, run the 16.15 leg-neutral gate, cap at 0.55.
+
+### 13i. A circulating number beats a long-run prior (from #24)
+#24 put 0.80 on 'TSMC Q2 gross margin below 65pct' to rebut a circulating 68pct claim, reasoning from foundry economics and TSMC's LONG-TERM margin guide. Print: 67.7pct (Q3 guide 65-67). It is the book's worst single score (0.64). A specific number circulating ahead of a print usually has a source (channel checks, a leaked preview, the company's own QUARTER guide); a structural prior about what margins 'should' be is not evidence about THIS quarter. RULE: before fading a pre-print number, pull (a) the company's guide FOR THAT QUARTER and (b) the prior quarter's actual. If the circulating number sits within ~2pts of either, do not fade it above 0.50. P >= 0.80 on a print requires the company's own quarter guide to exclude the alternative.
